## Why This Is Asked
Tests bridging a C object with Go, memory ownership, and safe cross-language concurrency using CGO. It also touches resource lifecycle (Close) and error handling when C returns NULL.

## Key Concepts
- CGO bindings for C structs and lifecycle
- Memory ownership and Free/Close semantics
- Thread-safety in C with a mutex vs. Go-level synchronization
- Cross-language error handling and result propagation

## Code Example
```c
// counter.h
typedef struct AtomicCounter AtomicCounter;
AtomicCounter* ac_new(void);
void ac_inc(AtomicCounter* ac);
int ac_get(AtomicCounter* ac);
void ac_free(AtomicCounter* ac);
```
```c
// counter.c (stub with mutex)
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
typedef struct AtomicCounter {
  int val;
  pthread_mutex_t m;
} AtomicCounter;
AtomicCounter* ac_new(void){ AtomicCounter* a = malloc(sizeof(AtomicCounter)); a->val=0; pthread_mutex_init(&a->m, NULL); return a; }
void ac_inc(AtomicCounter* a){ pthread_mutex_lock(&a->m); a->val++; pthread_mutex_unlock(&a->m); }
int ac_get(AtomicCounter* a){ pthread_mutex_lock(&a->m); int v=a->val; pthread_mutex_unlock(&a->m); return v; }
void ac_free(AtomicCounter* a){ pthread_mutex_destroy(&a->m); free(a); }
```
```go
// counter.go
package counter
/*
#include "counter.h"
#include <stdlib.h>
*/
import "C"
import (
  "errors"
)

type AtomicCounter struct{ c *C.AtomicCounter }

func NewAtomicCounter() (*AtomicCounter, error) {
  p := C.ac_new()
  if p == nil {
    return nil, errors.New("failed to create AtomicCounter")
  }
  return &AtomicCounter{c: p}, nil
}

func (a *AtomicCounter) Inc() { C.ac_inc(a.c) }
func (a *AtomicCounter) Get() int { return int(C.ac_get(a.c)) }
func (a *AtomicCounter) Close() { C.ac_free(a.c) }
